{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":8,"query":"name=Watson&limit=10&title=The Double Helix","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":[{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":[{"nonSort":{"@space":"preserve","#text":"The  "},"title":"annotated and illustrated double helix "},{"@type":"alternative","title":"Double helix"}],"name":[{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Watson, James D.,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1928-2025"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Gann, Alexander"},{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Witkowski, J. A. (Jan Anthony),",{"@type":"date","#text":"1947-"}]}],"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"biography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"History"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"dateIssued":[{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2012"},"c2012"],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Simon & Schuster"},"edition":"1st Simon & Schuster hardcover ed."},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xvi, 345 p. : ill., map ; 22 cm. cm."},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration"}},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"map"}}],"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"On the fiftieth anniversary of Watson and Crick receiving the Nobel Prize, a freshly annotated and illustrated edition of The Double Helix provides new insights into the personal relationships among James Watson, Frances Crick, Maurice Wilkins, Rosalind Franklin and a scientific revolution. In his 1968 memoir, The Double Helix, James Watson offered a thrilling drama of the race among scientists to identify the structure of DNA. Professors Alexander Gann and Jan Witkowski have built upon this narrative; juxtaposing Watsons' racy account with the commentary of other protagonists offering an enhanced perspective of the now legendary story. They have mined many sources: including a trove of newly discovered correspondence belonging to Francis Crick mislaid some fifty years earlier; excerpts from the papers of Maurice Wilkins, Linus Pauling, and Rosalind Franklin; and a chapter that had been dropped from the original. After half a century, the implications of the double helix keep rippling outward; the tools of molecular biology have forever transformed the life sciences. The New Annotated and Illustrated Edition of The Double Helix adds a richness to the account of the momentous events that led the charge. The Double Helix is the best book I know about a scientific discovery this new edition suffuses the whole with social history, fascinating documentation, photography, and cunning background research. The early fifties, the beginning of the modern age of molecular biology, spring to life. Ian McEwan, author of Atonement --Provided by publisher."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Preface to Annotated and Illustrated Edition -- Sir Lawrence Bragg's Foreword to the original edition -- Preface to the original edition -- Prologue from the original edition -- Chapters 1-29 -- Epilogue from the original edition -- The Nobel prize -- Appendix 1: The first letters describing the DNA model -- Appendix 2: The lost chapter from The Double Helix -- Appendix 3: Watson and the Merck Fellowship Board -- Appendix 4: Writing and publishing The Double Helix -- Appendix 5: Chargaff's review and the ensuing controversy."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"James D. Watson ; edited by Alexander Gann & Jan Witkowski."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and index."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Watson, James D."}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Watson, James D.,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1928-2025"}]}},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"DNA"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Genetic Code"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Molecular Biology"},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Watson, James D.,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1928-2025"}]},"topic":"Friends and associates"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["DNA","Research","History"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Genetic code","Research","History"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Molecular biology","Research","History"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Molecular biologists","genre":"Biography"},{"@authority":"gnd","name":{"@type":"corporate","namePart":"Design Network Switzerland"}}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"QH450.2 .W37 2012"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"QU 58.5"},{"@edition":"23","@authority":"ddc","#text":"572.8/6"}],"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Double helix"},"name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Watson, James D.,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1928-2025"}]}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990136106260203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9781476715490 (hardback)"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9781476715506 (pbk.)"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9781476715513 (ebook)"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^2012037483"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"810409777"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/000000046073724X","#text":"Cabot Science Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"QH450.2 .W37 2012"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Cabot Science"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990136106260203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"013610626"}},"processingDate":"2026-08-06T04:32Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"aacr","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"DNLM/DLC"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"120914"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20160410"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990136106260203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)"}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"nonSort":{"@space":"preserve","#text":"The  "},"title":"double helix ","subTitle":"a personal account of the discovery of the structure of DNA "},"name":[{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Watson, James D.,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1928-2025"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Viets, Henry R. (Henry Rouse),",{"@type":"date","#text":"1890-1969"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"former owner"}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Vidal, Gore,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1925-2012"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"former owner"}}},{"@type":"corporate","namePart":"Atheneum (New York, N.Y.)","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"publisher"}}}],"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"biography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"History"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"rbprov","#text":"Association copies (Provenance)"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Atheneum"},"dateIssued":"1968","edition":["1st ed."]},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xvi, 226 p. : ill., facsims., ports. ; 22 cm."},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration"}},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"facsimile"}},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"portrait"}}],"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"By identifying the structure of DNA, Francis Crick and James Watson revolutionized biochemistry and won a Nobel Prize. All the time Watson was only twenty-four, a young zoologist hungry to make his mark. His uncompromisingly honest account of the heady days of their thrilling sprint against other world-class researchers to solve one of sciences' greatest unsolved mysteries gives a dazzlingly clear picture of a world of scientists with great gifts, very human ambitions, and bitter rivalries. With humility unspoiled by false modesty, Watson relates his and Crick's desperate efforts to beat Linus Pauling to the identification of the basic building block of life."},"tableOfContents":[{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Diagrams: Short section of DNA, 1951 -- Chemical structures of the DNA bases, 1951 -- Covalent bonds of the sugar-phosphate backbone -- Schematic view of a nucleotide -- Mg** ions binding phosphate groups -- Schematic view of DNA, like-with-like base pairs -- Base pairs for the like-with-like structure -- Tautomeric forms of guanine and thymine -- Base pairs for the double helix -- Schematic illustration of the double helix -- DNA replication."},{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Crick and Watson, along the backs -- Francis in the Cavendish -- Maurice Wilkins: World wide photos -- The microbial genetics meeting, Copenhagen, March 1951 -- Linus Pauling: Information office, California Institute of Technology -- Sir Lawrence Bragg -- Rosalind Franklin -- X-ray diffraction photograph of DNA, A form -- Elizabeth Watson -- In Paris, Spring 1952 -- The meeting at Royaumont, July 1952 -- In the Italian Alps, August 1952 -- Early ideas on the DNA-RNA-protein relation -- X-ray diffraction photograph of DNA, B form -- Original model of the double helix -- Watson and Crick in front of the model: Photograph A. C. Barrington Brown -- Morning coffee in the Cavendish: Photograph A. C. Barrington Brown -- Letter to Max Delbruck -- In Stockholm, December 1962: Svenskt Pressfoto, Stockholm."}],"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"by James D. Watson."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"n-us---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Watson, James D.,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1928-2025"}]}},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"DNA"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Biochemistry","history"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"DNA"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Molecular biologists","geographic":"United States","genre":"Biography"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Genetic code"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Molecular biology","History"]},{"hierarchicalGeographic":{"country":"United States","state":"New York","city":"New York"}}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"QD341.A2 W315"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"QU 58 W339d 1968"},{"@authority":"ddc","#text":"547/.596"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Double helix; a personal account of the discovery of the structure of DNA"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"New York, Atheneum, 1968","edition":"[1st ed.]."},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)647654837"},"name":{"namePart":"Watson, James D., 1928-"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990006732710203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^68016217^//r89"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"439345"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel